---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Precision on a Budget — Shubhojit"
  description="A research hypothesis on using activation statistics to allocate limited high-precision, on-chip memory and improve inference quality–latency tradeoffs."
>
  <article>
    <div class="meta">Research note · Hypothesis</div>
    <h1>Precision on a Budget</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Can activation statistics help a model decide where scarce high-precision,
      on-chip memory is most valuable?
    </p>

    <p>
      Quantization changes more than the number of bits used to represent a
      weight. It changes which values move through the system, where temporary
      data lives, and how much work fits close to the compute units. That makes
      precision allocation a systems question as much as a numerical one.
    </p>

    <p>
      The idea here is a hypothesis, not a result: use information about the
      current activations to decide which parts of a computation deserve a
      higher-precision path, while keeping the fast-memory working set within a
      fixed budget.
    </p>

    <h2>The hypothesis</h2>
    <p>
      Under a fixed on-chip memory budget, activation-aware precision routing
      can achieve a better quality–latency tradeoff than a static outlier
      policy. In this framing, L1 cache is one possible budget; other hardware
      may expose a different fast-memory tier or capacity.
    </p>

    <div class="constraint" role="math" aria-label="Minimize end-to-end latency subject to task quality meeting a minimum target and the fast-memory working set staying within capacity C">
      <strong>minimize</strong> end-to-end latency<br />
      <strong>subject to</strong> task quality ≥ Q<sub>min</sub><br />
      fast-memory working set ≤ C
    </div>

    <p>
      The central question is whether the activation distribution can guide
      the allocation of scarce high-precision and on-chip memory resources
      better than a fixed rule can.
    </p>

    <h2>What would be routed?</h2>
    <p>
      A layer could divide its computation into units such as channels or
      blocks. An allocator would estimate which units are most sensitive to
      reduced precision for the current input, then spend a limited high-
      precision budget on those units. The rest would use a lower-precision
      path. The exact signal and granularity are open design choices: activation
      magnitude is easy to measure, but magnitude alone need not predict
      quantization error or downstream task quality.
    </p>

    <p>
      “Activation-aware” also needs a precise meaning. Routing could adapt for
      every input, operate at batch or block granularity, or use statistics
      gathered during calibration. These choices trade responsiveness against
      the cost of measuring, packing, and dispatching the selected values.
    </p>

    <h2>How to test it</h2>
    <p>
      Compare dynamic allocation with uniform low-precision execution and a
      clearly specified static outlier baseline. Hold the model, task data, and
      total fast-memory budget constant. Sweep that budget and measure the
      quality–latency frontier, rather than reporting a single point chosen
      after the fact.
    </p>

    <p>At minimum, report:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Task quality at each memory budget.</li>
      <li>End-to-end latency, including detection, packing, dispatch, and result fusion.</li>
      <li>Working-set size and hardware counters relevant to cache or on-chip-memory behavior.</li>
      <li>Results across input lengths and batch sizes, not only one favorable workload.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The comparison should include the overhead of routing. A policy that
      saves cache capacity but adds irregular gathers, extra launches, or
      synchronization may lose on latency. Likewise, fitting a nominal working
      set under C does not guarantee that it will avoid conflict misses or
      achieve better bandwidth on a real processor.
    </p>

    <h2>What could make the idea fail?</h2>
    <p>
      The activation signal may not identify the values that matter most to
      output quality. Routing overhead may erase the savings. Dynamic sparsity
      may interfere with vectorized kernels, or the selected high-precision
      subset may change too quickly to reuse data effectively. Any of these
      outcomes would be useful: they would show where static quantization or a
      simpler allocation rule is the better systems choice.
    </p>

    <h2>Scope and prior work</h2>
    <p>
      This is not a claim that mixed-precision outlier handling is new.
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.07339" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LLM.int8()</a>
      uses a mixed-precision decomposition for outlier features, while
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.10438" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">SmoothQuant</a>
      uses activation statistics to shift quantization difficulty between
      activations and weights. The question here is narrower and should be
      tested against relevant existing methods: can the allocation policy be
      explicitly optimized for a constrained fast-memory budget, and does that
      improve end-to-end quality and latency?
    </p>

    <p>
      This is one possible extension of the broader token-efficiency argument.
      Shorter sequences reduce the amount of work; precision routing asks how
      efficiently each unit of work is represented and moved. Both matter, but
      they are different budgets—and should be measured separately.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

